} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "5,000 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 8 February 2026 — 16:00"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2201"} "Milano Speed Skating Stadium, Fiera Milano Rho, Rho, Milano, ITA"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/15001103"} "20 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record "] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/19020403"} "6:08.84"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/138219"} "Nils van der Poel"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"] " /  6 February 2022 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/533"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/1807430"} "Berri de Jonge"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Starter"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700024"} "Deng Xuefeng"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Beijing Olympic champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/138219"} "Nils van der Poel"] " retired after his Olympic victory, and changed his surname to return to anonymity. " [:a {:href "/athletes/137696"} "Patrick Roest"] ", silver medallist in 2022 then claimed the 2023-24 World titles, ahead of Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/137427"} "Davide Ghiotto"] ". But Roest suffered from fatigue following pericoronitis since 2024, and was only allowed to take part in the Dutch Olympic trials on a wildcard. Ghiotto seemed the logical successor to Roest, but a host of other contenders appeared in 2025. " [:a {:href "/athletes/140169"} "Sander Eitrem"] " of Norway earned the 2025 World title, and just two weeks before Milano Cortina, he broke a magical barrier by becoming the first skater to complete the 5,000 m in less than 6 minutes: 5:58.52. Earlier in the year, France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/156505"} "Timothy Loubineaud"] " had already bettered vder Poel’s world record. The 2025/26 long distance World Cup was won by the young Czech talent " [:a {:href "/athletes/156326"} "Metoděj Jílek"] ", who had also won one 5,000 m World Cup, just as " [:a {:href "/athletes/149164"} "Casey Dawson"] " (USA). Absent in the race was the 2026 European Champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/156989"} "Vladimir Semirunniy"] ". The Polish skater, bronze medallist at the 2025 Worlds, had recorded poor performances at the first few World Cups and had only managed to qualify for a reserve position."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The first rider to beat 6:10 came in the third pair, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/156754"} "Riccardo Lorello"] ". The Italian, hailing from Rho, location of the Milano Speed Skating Stadium, had won a surprise silver at the 2026 European Championships, and now clocked 6:09.22. Other competitors had a hard time beating Lorello’s time, and even his more experience compatriot Ghiotto came up short despite clocking faster times nearly all of the race – 6:09.57. In pair nine, Eitrem and Jílek started at the same pace as Lorello. Around 2,000 m, Eitrem pulled away from Jílek and Lorello’s splits. The Norwegian held on to 28-second laps up until 4,600 m, and finished in 6:03.95, a serious improvement of Van der Poel’s Olympic Record from 2022, as well as a new unofficial low-altitude world record. In the final pair Loubineaud was never quite able to threaten the leading times, finishing in 6:11.15 and fifth place."] "\n\n" [:p {} "For the first time since 1984, no Dutch skaters were on the 5,000 m podium . Conversely, Eitrem’s gold marked the first Norwegian gold in the event since " [:a {:href "/athletes/88548"} "Johann Olav Koss"] " in 1994. Jílek’s became Czechia’s first male speed skater to earn an Olympic medal, and third overall after " [:a {:href "/athletes/109768"} "Martina Sáblíková"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/119682"} "Karolína Erbanová"] ", while Lorello’s surprise bronze was the second medal for Italy in the event, the first having come at the previous Olympics in Italy, 2006."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button927707", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button927707').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "600 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1000 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1400 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1800 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "2200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "2600 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "3000 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "3400 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "3800 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "4200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "4600 m"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "9O"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/140169"} "Sander Eitrem"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "6:03.95"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "19.37"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "47.74"] [:td {:class "split", :s